thread:
sam: Heads up — intermittent 504s from our Next middleware when calling the SDK on Vercel Edge. Local dev + node serverless are fine, only fails when deployed to edge.

maya: Same. Symptoms: request hangs for ~10s then returns 504. No stacktrace from the middleware, just the edge runtime logs show a timeout.

leo: Could be the bundler pulling in a node-only auth helper or a require() shim. Edge bundles can't have dynamic requires or native node libs.

arjun: I dug in — the default import path can resolve to the CJS/node build depending on tooling. Next's SWC sometimes prefers the CJS path unless exports are explicit.

maya: Short workaround that fixes our prod routes: explicitly import the edge entry point and pass globalThis.fetch + an AbortController timeout. Example: 
```
import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference/edge.mjs'
export function middleware(req) {
  const ac = new AbortController()
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 7000)
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API, fetch: globalThis.fetch })
  try {
    const res = await client.embeddings.create({ input: 'x' , signal: ac.signal })
    return NextResponse.next()
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timeout)
  }
}
```
This avoids node-only helpers and gives a deterministic cancel path.

talia: FYI timers are weird on some edge RTEs — setTimeout exists but you shouldn't rely on long-running timers. AbortController is more portable. Also recommend the SDK not call setTimeout from module init code (that breaks cold-start in edge).

sam: Can we make the package consumer-friendly so devs don't have to know the edge import? Adding an explicit `exports` entry for an edge file would help. Something like: 
```json
"exports": {
  "./edge": {"import":"./dist/edge.mjs", "default":"./dist/edge.mjs"},
  ".": {"import":"./dist/index.mjs","require":"./dist/index.cjs"}
}
```
Then docs recommend `import from 'redwood-inference/edge'` in middleware.

leo: Also watch TypeScript defs. Our bundled d.ts references NodeJS types (Buffer, fs) which cause the typechecker to pull in node types into edge-only apps. We should ship a slim edge.d.ts without Node references and point `types` or provide `typesVersions` mapping.

maya: Filed GH issue: sdk#452 with repro steps and a tiny Next middleware repo (link: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/452). Repro steps: create pages/_middleware.ts, import default SDK, call text generation, deploy to Vercel Edge.

ci-bot: Canary build started for branch fix/edge-exports (canary-id: canary-2026-03-21-12).

arjun: Another gotcha — process.env is sometimes statically replaced by the bundler but process.* references can be removed by tree-shaking only if they're not used at runtime. Avoid reading process at module top-level. Use lazy access inside functions.

talia: For DX: let's add an examples/nextjs-middleware-edge/ minimal repo and a short README snippet to the SDK README showing the import, passing global fetch, AbortController usage, and a note about not using default import in middleware. That should reduce onboarding friction.

sam: Sounds good. I'll smoke-test the canary after CI finishes and try the example locally with Vercel CLI.

leo: To debug bundle resolution locally, this is handy: `node -p \"require.resolve('redwood-inference')\"` and `node -p \"require.resolve('redwood-inference/edge')\"` — and run `npx why redwood-inference` in the .next build to see the dependency tree.

maya: Thanks folks. Plan = (1) land edge export + edge d.ts, (2) remove node-top-level shims, (3) switch retry to AbortController-friendly backoff, (4) add example + README note. Will post PR links here.

sam: Ping when canary is live and I'll retest. :rocket:
